# Useful commands — Remus.Mvc

All commands are run from the project root:

    cd /Users/mim/Sites/dotnet_mvc


## Running the app

    dotnet run                          # http profile  -> http://localhost:5260
    dotnet run --launch-profile https   # https profile -> https://localhost:7080
    dotnet watch                        # run with hot reload on file save

The profiles and their ports are defined in Properties/launchSettings.json.
Both set ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development.

Stop the app with Ctrl-C.

This one app serves BOTH the MVC pages and the JSON API under /api. There is no
second project to start. If you are working on the Angular client, see "The
Angular client" below - you run this app AND the Angular dev server, and you
open the Angular one.


## Build

    dotnet restore      # fetch NuGet packages
    dotnet build        # compile without running
    dotnet clean        # wipe bin/ and obj/


## HTTPS certificate

Only needed the first time the https profile is used, if the browser or the
runtime complains about an untrusted certificate:

    dotnet dev-certs https --trust


## Secrets (connection string, password)

The Postgres connection string is NOT in the repo. It lives in .NET's
user-secrets store, keyed by the UserSecretsId in Remus.Mvc.csproj:

    ~/.microsoft/usersecrets/52888986-e459-4a61-88f3-b915cf6c7fd3/secrets.json

which resolves to:

    /Users/mim/.microsoft/usersecrets/52888986-e459-4a61-88f3-b915cf6c7fd3/secrets.json

It is plain, unencrypted JSON kept outside the project directory so it cannot
be committed. This is a development-only mechanism; in production use
environment variables or a secrets manager instead.

    dotnet user-secrets list       # show every secret for this project
    dotnet user-secrets set "ConnectionStrings:DefaultConnection" "Host=localhost;Port=5432;Database=remus_dotnet;Username=postgres;Password=YOUR_PASSWORD"
    dotnet user-secrets remove "ConnectionStrings:DefaultConnection"
    dotnet user-secrets clear      # remove all of them

At runtime these merge into IConfiguration on top of appsettings.json, which is
why GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection") in Program.cs finds the value even
though appsettings.json has no ConnectionStrings section.


## Users and roles

Create an admin user from THIS project (Django: manage.py createsuperuser).
Prompts for anything not given on the command line:

    dotnet run -- createsuperuser
    dotnet run -- createsuperuser boss@remus.local
    dotnet run -- createsuperuser boss@remus.local 'Str0ng!Pass'
    dotnet run -- createsuperuser boss@remus.local 'Str0ng!Pass' --roles Admin,SysMan
    dotnet run -- createsuperuser --help

Prefer the prompting form: a password passed as an argument lands in your
shell history. The command creates the user, confirms its email, creates its
profile with IsValidated = true, and assigns Admin + SysMan + BasicUser. It
never starts the web server, and exits 0 on success, 1 on refusal, 2 on bad
usage.

Passwords must satisfy the rules in Program.cs: at least 8 characters, with an
uppercase, a lowercase, a digit and a non-alphanumeric character.

Roles are read, never created. They live in the shared AspNetRoles table and
are seeded by the Blazor app. Known roles: Admin, SysMan, CbtOperator,
OsiOperator, ScientificOperator, BasicUser.

Inspect who exists and what they hold:

    PGPASSWORD='<password>' psql -h localhost -U postgres -d remus_dotnet -c \
    'SELECT u."UserName", string_agg(r."Name", '"'"', '"'"') AS roles
     FROM "AspNetUsers" u
     LEFT JOIN "AspNetUserRoles" ur ON ur."UserId" = u."Id"
     LEFT JOIN "AspNetRoles" r ON r."Id" = ur."RoleId"
     GROUP BY u."UserName" ORDER BY u."UserName";'

Role changes only take effect at the next sign-in: roles are written into the
auth cookie at login, not read from the database on each request.

Passwords cannot be changed with SQL - the PasswordHash column holds a PBKDF2
hash that Identity generates.


## Database

This project does not own the schema: there is no Migrations folder and
Database.Migrate() is never called (see the note in Program.cs). The Blazor app
owns the schema and runs the migrations. These are read-only checks:

    pg_isready                                          # is Postgres up?
    psql -h localhost -U postgres -l                    # list databases
    psql -h localhost -U postgres -d remus_dotnet       # open a shell on this database

Inside psql:  \dt  list tables   \d <table>  describe a table   \q  quit


## The API  (/api)

The same app also answers JSON under /api, for the Angular client in
../remus-angular. Same port, same process, same .Remus.Mvc.Auth cookie: sign in
on the MVC pages and the API already knows who you are.

    GET   /api/auth/me       who you are. Anonymous callers get 200, not 401.
    POST  /api/auth/login    sign in; the response carries the auth cookie
    POST  /api/auth/logout   clear it
    GET   /api/profile       the signed-in user's profile
    PUT   /api/profile       replace its editable fields

The endpoints live in Api/ and are MINIMAL APIs, not controllers - the routes
are explicit strings in Api/AuthEndpoints.cs and Api/ProfileEndpoints.cs rather
than being implied by class and method names the way Controllers/ are. The
comment at the top of Api/ProfileEndpoints.cs compares the two styles.

The machine-readable description of all of it (Development only):

    curl -s http://localhost:5260/openapi/v1.json | python3 -m json.tool

Note it is OpenAPI **3.1.1**. .NET 10 emits 3.1 by default and a fair number of
client generators still only read 3.0, which produces confusing "unsupported
version" errors. If you hit one, either pick a generator that handles 3.1 (see
below) or downgrade the document in Program.cs:

    builder.Services.AddOpenApi(options =>
        options.OpenApiVersion = Microsoft.OpenApi.OpenApiSpecVersion.OpenApi3_0);


### Calling it from the shell

Two things make curl harder than a browser here, and both are deliberate.

  1. Authentication is a COOKIE, so you need a cookie jar: -c writes it,
     -b sends it.
  2. Every write needs a CSRF token. A GET under /api issues the XSRF-TOKEN
     cookie; the write echoes its value back in the X-XSRF-TOKEN header.
     Angular's HttpClient does this unprompted. curl does not.

    # 1. any GET, to get a jar with a token in it
    curl -s -c /tmp/remus.jar http://localhost:5260/api/auth/me

    # 2. read the token out of the jar
    TOKEN=$(awk '/XSRF-TOKEN/ {print $7}' /tmp/remus.jar)

    # 3. sign in - the jar now also holds .Remus.Mvc.Auth
    curl -s -b /tmp/remus.jar -c /tmp/remus.jar -X POST \
         http://localhost:5260/api/auth/login \
         -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
         -H "X-XSRF-TOKEN: $TOKEN" \
         -d '{"email":"admin@remus.local","password":"YOUR_PASSWORD","rememberMe":false}'

    # 4. authenticated calls now work
    curl -s -b /tmp/remus.jar http://localhost:5260/api/profile | python3 -m json.tool

    # 5. a write
    curl -s -b /tmp/remus.jar -X PUT http://localhost:5260/api/profile \
         -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
         -H "X-XSRF-TOKEN: $TOKEN" \
         -d '{"firstName":"Ada","lastName":"Lovelace","country":"dz"}'

Trigger each of these failures once, so you recognise them later:

    400  {"title":"Invalid or missing antiforgery token."}
             step 1 or 2 was skipped. Api/ApiSetup.cs explains the mechanism.

    400  {"errors":{"FirstName":["First name is required."]}}
             the body failed validation. Note the key is "FirstName" with a
             capital F while every other property in every other response is
             camelCase - see the note in remus-angular's problem-details.ts.

    401  {"title":"Sign-in failed."}
             wrong credentials. The same answer for a locked-out or
             unconfirmed account, on purpose - AuthEndpoints.cs says why.

    401  (empty body)
             not signed in. Compare with the same request to /Profile, which
             gets 302 to /Account/Login instead:

                 curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://localhost:5260/api/profile
                 curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://localhost:5260/Profile

             401 versus 302 is a deliberate split configured in Program.cs, and
             it is the fix for the trap documented in wwwroot/js/profile-edit.js.


## The Angular client  (../remus-angular)

A second front end over that API. Separate npm project, same level as this one:

    /Users/mim/Sites/
      dotnet_mvc/       MVC pages + /api     :5260    (this project)
      remus-angular/    Angular 22           :4200
      remus_dotnet/     Blazor               :5199    owns the schema

Needs Node. v22.23 is installed here; Angular 22 wants v20.19+ or v22.12+.

### First time

    cd ../remus-angular
    npm install

That resolves from the existing package-lock.json and takes a few seconds.

If you ever delete the lockfile, the fresh resolve currently crashes npm 10.9.8
with "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'edgesOut')". It is a bug in
npm's peer-dependency resolver around vitest, not in Angular. Two ways out:

    npm install --legacy-peer-deps      # skips strict peer resolution
    npm install -g npm@11               # fixes it properly

The project is not a git repository yet. `git init` there if you want one; it
is deliberately separate from this repo, the same way remus_dotnet is.

### Day to day - TWO terminals, both running

    # terminal 1 - the API
    cd /Users/mim/Sites/dotnet_mvc && dotnet watch

    # terminal 2 - the client
    cd /Users/mim/Sites/remus-angular && npm start

Then open http://localhost:4200 - NOT :5260. The Angular dev server serves the
page and forwards everything under /api to :5260 (proxy.conf.mjs). Opening
:5260 gives you the MVC pages, which is the point of keeping both.

Forget terminal 1 and the page loads with "Cannot reach the server" - nothing
is broken, the proxy simply has nothing to forward to. Start the API and
reload.

    npm start        ng serve on :4200, hot reload     Django: runserver
    npm run build    production bundle into dist/
    npm test         vitest, once
    npx ng generate component pages/foo                scaffold a component

`ng` is not on the PATH: the CLI is installed per project. Use the npm scripts,
or `npx ng <anything>`.

### Generating the client instead of hand-writing it

src/app/core/api.models.ts is written by hand as an exercise. When it stops
teaching you anything, generate it from the OpenAPI document instead - with the
MVC app running:

    cd ../remus-angular
    npx ng-openapi-gen --input http://localhost:5260/openapi/v1.json \
                       --output src/app/api

ng-openapi-gen reads OpenAPI 3.1, which matters (see the note above). It emits
typed models plus Angular services built on HttpClient. NSwag is the more
common .NET-shop answer and produces similar output, but its npm package ships
.NET 8 binaries and there is no .NET 8 runtime on this machine - it fails with
"You must install or update .NET" until you install one.

A generated client cannot drift from the server. A hand-written one drifts the
first time somebody renames a C# property, and nothing tells you.


## Port conflicts

If a port is already taken ("address already in use"):

    lsof -nP -iTCP:5260 -sTCP:LISTEN     # what is holding the http port
    lsof -nP -iTCP:7080 -sTCP:LISTEN     # what is holding the https port
    lsof -nP -iTCP:4200 -sTCP:LISTEN     # what is holding the Angular dev server
    kill <PID>

If 5260 is taken, remember that proxy.conf.mjs in the Angular project hard-codes
it. Moving the API means editing that file too, or every /api call 504s.


